the idea to make a movie inspired by The  Snow Queen the Hans Christian Andersen  fairy tale has been percolating at  Disney for a while well I know that Walt  always wanted to do it we really didn't  see any transcripts or anything I don't  know I don't know if there any left but  I think it was what was it 1939 that we  found out and there was actually a  production number given to the to the  show so and 1092 yes we know these facts  now and but I didn't know during the  making of it and we just saw we were  just with Alice Davis yesterday looking  at some artwork that Mark Davis had done  and it was actually for more of an  attraction at Disneyland ice palace yes  so so it's been kicking around there was  they tried some versions in the early I  think late 90s early 2000s I came back  in 2008 came back to Disney and John  Lasseter asked me to come back and it  was one of the projects that was you  know still there and it just intrigued  me with the environment which I thought  was amazing it's already magical you've  got the ice and snow which is so  beautiful and Disney never done a full  feature in the ice and snow and then the  the character self The Snow Queen who is  so intriguing she's not really very well  defined in the action in the Hans  Christian Andersen's story so but there  were a lot of questions about that and  that's where we we sort of played a  little bit more with that so that sort  of worked started I had been a writer on  wreck-it Ralph and that's how I came to  Disney and so I was very familiar used  to being in the story room and writers  are in the editorial rooms as well and  and they work with the actors - so is it  very comfortable and I love frozen I  what we do at Disney is we sort of help  each other with their move each other's  movies we go in and give notes every few  weeks they put up the film and we'll  look at it and give notes and I was  really falling for frozen and giving  notes and and Peter and Chris could see  I was connecting with  and asked as I was almost done or  through if I would come on and and write  frozen and I was delighted and then John  and Edie kind of watching how we all  work together and watching Chris and I  particularly felt like we made a great  team and you know we have very intense  schedule and they asked if I would code  rec with Chris and in that is rare I'm  the first writer to do that normally  it's an animator who works many many  years up and to become a director but I  think we were just such a good team  story-wise I could keep that going and  he could keep the animation going if  needed and it just it just worked out  for us we're very fortunate Kristen  anderson-lopez great lyricist Award  winner and Bobby Lopez of course won a  Tony Award for Book of Mormon and Avenue  Q and since I had worked with them  previously was very familiar with them  and when Chris said that he wanted this  to be a musical we wanted to embrace  that and the reason I think not only did  Chris enjoy going to New York and seeing  those productions but we knew that it  was really important to have writers on  from the very very beginning of the  project so I joined two and a half years  ago I believe they joined probably about  two years ago and we're in every  everyday we met with them for at least  two hours a lot of times it was via  videoconference because they were in New  York but we talked about character we  talked about story we talked about the  arc of the characters long before they  wrote any songs and that was really  important because we always knew we  wanted the songs to do one of two things  either tell you something about the  character that they that you didn't know  before or to propel the story forward  and to do that they had to be intimately  involved with the story process we  wanted to integrate the characters and  they don't just walk on snow they're  inside of it they're falling in it  they're walking through two feet of snow  and we wanted to create ice that was  powerful and emotional because it's it's  created magically by Elsa and to do that  the technology was not there before at  all we had to build it in the very  beginning the snow would sort of clump  like packing peanuts and they built some  incredible stuff we once called the  Matterhorn which is very Disney there's  a snow batcher and and then they also  with the ice we found  you could do glass or plastic looking  ice very easily but like humans ice is  very flawed and that's what makes it  beautiful and so we had to like hand go  in and create flaws so the ice felt like  ice and we must say we don't go for  realism I think when you go for realism  and animation it can pull you out but we  go for believability and in doing that  it's a feeling you can feel when it's  it's not ice to you and and so the team  pushed harder than they ever had that  technology took many many many hours to  render on the computer it was tough and  we did our research too we did a lot of  research we sent Peter didn't send us  but our but our artists got to go to an  ice hotel first  there's one in Quebec City again we saw  how the light would come in and refract  and reflect off of the ice and and the  the team did some they took some  beautiful videos and pictures and that  really inspired them for Elsa's ice  palace and then they also went to our  animators went to Wyoming to study what  it would be like to actually walk in  with that big skirt that on a walks in  in the snow and the men and the women  animators we only you know they got to  walk through it and that was tough but  we got a lot of great video and they  felt it they really when you're doing  animation it's good if you've actually  experienced something like that and then  you guys have talked about the other  trip the big big trip was to Norway and  and a lot of that you know is  environment and that's a big part but  also the culture it's the balance  technology-wise you know we wanted to  we've got more simulated cloth in this  film than any film all our films  together combined and it was the  discovery of the intimacy of the  Norwegian folk art and the stave  churches and the Rose mulling and  balancing that against the Giants know a  few words which it was the perfect  landscape we could create intimacy and  intimate setting with the characters and  then put them at this adventure that was  giant and scope and and very treacherous  and it was the greatest inspiration I  think for us for the film and at the  beginning of  the film you probably also heard a very  curious song that's unlike anything  we've done and that's actually Norwegian  joy King a lot of people don't know  which is a folk singing that's done and  so we were just really inspired by that  in a huge way and our technologies team  said well let's let's take from it and  build everything we can we do 3d at the  exact same time we're making the the 2d  version of the movie so it's side by  side and Mike GMO our art director is  very detail-oriented and and I would say  he uses a lot of color thanks Colin he  does like color but what was great was  he actually used the color to evoke the  emotion of the character so it was  grounded in the emotion and a really  amazing color palette I knew that this  story could be big and could could  actually be great as a musical but I  also wanted to do a musical eye the  other movies I've done you know Tarzan I  would say it's a movie with songs it's  not really musical and surfs up had  music behind it but then this was my  chance to do do a real musical where  characters were actually singing so that  was I was personally why I wanted to do  it but I think because of it the epic  scale and everything I think it fits  beautifully there's a lot that you  needed to do and what songs allow you to  do is particularly the way we did it  with a true musical you it carries you  through the plot and emotions it doesn't  stop and then there's a song and then  you keep going there's always some  storytelling that enhances it and there  are things that Bobby and Kristen could  do with a song that as a screenwriter  would take me five or six scenes to  develop we'd have a miniseries if it was  if it weren't a musical I think and so  it was for me it was just being able to  reach emotionally and and an  entertainment entertainment level that  would take a lot more as a writer to do  was it was heaven to work with him and  Bobby and Kristen would always tease her  saying we're gonna take your best scenes  and want to turn those into songs and  those songs will be ours we often talked  about that songs in many ways can touch  the soul a lot faster and a lot more a  lot deeper sometimes than the dialogue  scene can do Adina I don't I don't think  we actually asked her to sing a song the  funny the funny thing there was when we  were asking  Bobby and Kristen what kind of songs do  they think they're gonna write for the  movies so we could tell that whoever was  gonna audition what type of song to  bring in we noticed that almost all the  songs idina menzel had sung at one point  in her life so she sort of rose to the  top pretty quickly what was amazing that  Adina first though is you know she has  one of the most powerful voices but her  speaking voice has a gentleness and a  slight vulnerability and that's exactly  what we needed for Elsa so we didn't  even know it at the time because we were  in the early development we weren't sure  if she was gonna be a full villain or  not and we were trying to push toward  villain and she just was a much more  complex interesting person to work with  and Elsa became much more complex and  interesting and they could to me there  could be no other Elsa it just she  brought everything that was that balance  and then of course we had Kristen Bell  which it was amazing surprise first that  she could sing but she is you know  brilliant wonderfully funny a great  partner and we actually improved a lot  with her because she like me thinks  girls can be funny and so we decided to  really just go for it and have him you  know have a lot of fun with making her  just a real girl and the other guys they  all had musical chops jonathan groff who  Broadway Spring Awakening and lots of  other musicals and amazing talent he  actually we were looking for I was  looking for a voice that was a little  more gruff more Mountain Man esque and a  man a few words kind of character and he  came in and kind of changed it he was so  charming and just this very warm lovely  voice and I finished the audition with  him I was in the room with him reading  off reading the lines and then I came  back into the booth and he had left and  everyone the women and the men were  swooning and so it was it was that was  kind of a slam dunk right of course Josh  then Josh Book of Mormon you know we  knew he could sing quite well and have  fun and then he was one of the first  ones to come in also we loved his voice  and what he created for that we also  have sent Tina Fontana who is now  playing Prince Charming in on Broadway  in Cinderella  again a great great voice and a great  singer and he came in with an audition  he changed the lyrics to I feel pretty -  I am pretty and he's saying it was it  was terrific so we had a lot of fun with  all yeah
 

